\noindent
Dear Editor and Reviewers,

We sincerely thank you for your careful evaluation of our manuscript and for the constructive and insightful comments. We acknowledge a central limitation of the original submission: although the manuscript demonstrated that several predefined workflows could be executed successfully, it did not provide sufficient platform-level evidence for the claim that research intent could be reliably translated into an appropriate workflow, and several statements exceeded the strength of the evidence.

In response to the central concern raised by Reviewer \#4, the revised manuscript has been repositioned from a proof-of-concept workflow demonstration to a constrained workflow-platform validation study, and all claims have been restricted to the evaluated workflow space.

The major revisions are summarised as follows:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*, label=\arabic*.]
    \item We repositioned the manuscript as a workflow-platform validation study; the title, abstract, and conclusions now present DentalClaw as an orchestration and validation layer rather than as a new segmentation model or a clinical decision-support system.

    \item We added a prespecified six-dimension intent-to-workflow decision evaluation over 30 natural-language requests spanning four behavior classes (5 standard executable requests, 13 boundary requests without a validated route, 7 ambiguous requests, and 5 trap requests), with the expected outcome of each request defined before evaluation; the study-defined weighted decision score was 0.958 (all 30 requests above the 0.70 pass threshold), and no unvalidated workflow was silently executed. Under the same rubric, three general-purpose assistants (Copilot, Qwen, and Claude Code) scored 0.889--0.893 on the same 30 requests; because one assistant used the same reasoning backend as the platform, the gap is attributable to the platform's deterministic routing architecture rather than to model capability.

    \item We documented the five-stage agent decision pipeline and a fail-safe execution policy: only methods present in the offline registry can be executed automatically, external proposals require explicit user confirmation, and all workflow steps are performed by local, versioned execution modules rather than by the reasoning agent.

    \item We expanded the dataset descriptions with case counts and dominant exclusion reasons, annotation provenance for the public datasets, clinical-expert annotations and ethics approval for the private dataset, and deterministic case-level splitting with duplicate-identifier leakage checks.

    \item We clarified the reasoning-backend configuration (low sampling temperature, structured JSON decisions with a parsing fallback), the auxiliary role of literature search, and the data-handling boundary (only textual task descriptors and registry metadata are sent to the external model API; raw images, annotations, and patient identifiers remain local).

    \item We added an audit-trail description covering the plan file, QC decisions, execution logs, and generated outputs, and we reframed reproducibility claims as design intent supported by traceability evidence.

    \item We revised clinical terminology throughout (researcher-oriented, review-oriented) and removed or qualified claims not supported by evidence, including lowering the technical barrier, clinician-facing design, and efficiency superiority of the manual comparison, which is now reported as a workflow demonstration rather than a controlled human-factor experiment.

    \item We cited nnU-Net, MONAI, and 3D Slicer separately, added references to two recent benchmarks of agent decision evaluation (WildClawBench and OpenClawBench) to motivate the evaluation protocol, and expanded the registry specifications so that each validated route explicitly declares its required annotation type, evaluation unit, evaluation metrics, quality-control gates, and contraindications.

    \item We comprehensively revised the manuscript for English grammar, academic style, syntax, concision, and consistency.
\end{enumerate}

All revisions have been identified in the revised manuscript. The page and line numbers in this response will be updated after the final line-numbered version of the revised manuscript has been prepared.

