\section*{Response to Reviewer \#4}
% =========================================================

\ReviewerComment{
The platform-level claims are not adequately supported by the evidence. The current results show only that the implemented workflows can be executed, but do not demonstrate that the system can reliably translate natural-language research intent into an appropriate analysis plan, data structure, model pathway, reference standard, and validation strategy.
}

\AuthorResponse{
We agree with this central criticism. The revised manuscript no longer treats the successful execution of two predefined segmentation workflows as sufficient evidence of platform validity.

We have refocused the study on the intent-to-workflow process and added a prespecified six-dimension decision evaluation over 30 prespecified requests. This evaluation directly assesses whether the platform selects the registered method for supported requests, rejects out-of-scope requests, asks for clarification on ambiguous or underspecified requests, and routes requests without a validated route to an external proposal that requires explicit human approval.

The two-dimensional and three-dimensional segmentation experiments have therefore been repositioned as task-level execution case studies rather than as the primary evidence supporting platform validity.
}

\Location{
Title; Abstract; Introduction; Methods, Sections 2.1--2.5; Results, Sections 3.1--3.3; Discussion; Conclusion.
}

\RevisedText{
The objective of this study was to assess whether a researcher-oriented workflow platform could reduce the coordination burden of multi-step dental imaging AI research by transforming natural-language instructions into executable, reviewable, and traceable research pipelines.
}

\ReviewerComment{
The correctness, robustness, reproducibility, ambiguity handling, and failure modes of the intent-to-workflow process itself have not been evaluated.
}

\AuthorResponse{
We added a dedicated evaluation of the intent-to-workflow process itself, reported in Section 3.2 and Table 1 of the revised manuscript.

The evaluation covers 30 prespecified requests in four behavior classes: 5 standard executable requests, 13 boundary requests without a validated route, 7 ambiguous requests, and 5 trap (out-of-scope or quality-control-first) requests, spanning three dataset groups and four task families; several requests deliberately omitted the task family from the request text. Each request received a score computed as a weighted combination of six prespecified dimensions: planning (0.35), QC blocking (0.20), intent parsing (0.15), external proposal quality (0.15), ambiguity handling (0.10), and boundary identification (0.05); the full per-request results are provided in Supplementary Table S5.

The platform routed all 5 standard requests to the correct registry method; all 25 remaining requests were blocked without execution (11 non-executable external proposals and 2 rejections for boundary requests, 4 clarifications and 3 proposals for ambiguous requests, and 2 clarifications and 3 proposals for trap requests). The overall study-defined weighted decision score was 0.958, with all 30 requests above the 0.70 pass threshold, and no unvalidated workflow was silently executed.

To contextualize this result, the same 30 requests were issued to three general-purpose assistants (Copilot, Qwen, and Claude Code) under the same rubric. The assistants scored 0.889--0.893 overall and, on ambiguous requests, consistently guessed and routed instead of requesting clarification, with all three failing the same two ambiguous requests. Because the Claude Code comparison used the same reasoning backend as the platform (DeepSeek V4 Pro), this gap is attributable to the platform's deterministic registry lookup and conservative clarification policy rather than to model capability. The protocol follows recent practice in native-runtime agent evaluation (WildClawBench, OpenClawBench), in which deterministic rule-based checks against prespecified expectations are preferred over task-oracle success alone.

We also explicitly state in the Discussion that the intent set was constructed by the study team rather than drawn from a standardized public benchmark, and that the dimension weights reflect study-defined priorities rather than externally validated consensus.
}

\Location{
Methods, Section 2.5; Results, Section 3.2, Table 1, and Supplementary Table S5; Discussion.
}

\RevisedText{
Each intent received a score computed as a weighted combination of six prespecified dimensions: planning (0.35), QC blocking (0.20), intent parsing (0.15), external proposal quality (0.15), ambiguity handling (0.10), and boundary identification (0.05). The overall score was then computed as the average of the per-intent scores, with intents scoring at or above 0.70 considered passing.
}

\ReviewerComment{
The manuscript conflates standardised evaluation with appropriate evaluation. Automatically calculating a metric does not establish that the workflow or metric is appropriate for the research question, data unit, annotation type, clinical or methodological purpose, or potential sources of bias.
}

\AuthorResponse{
We agree that automatically computing a metric does not by itself establish methodological appropriateness. The revised manuscript documents the safeguards that operate before execution and the conditions under which a decision is considered correct.

The built-in quality-control stage checks image completeness, annotation integrity, metadata consistency, and train--test leakage risk, including duplicate-identifier verification across splits, and the splits themselves are generated deterministically at the case level with a fixed random seed. Requests with insufficient information are routed to clarification rather than to an unsupported assumption, and a decision is considered correct only when the selected route matches the intended dataset, task family, modality, and execution mode, or when the request is correctly rejected.

Methodological appropriateness is additionally encoded before execution in the registry specifications: each validated entry explicitly declares its required annotation type, evaluation unit, evaluation metrics, required quality-control gates, and contraindications (for example, a segmentation route declares pixel-mask annotations, image-level evaluation, and Dice/IoU metrics, and contraindicates bounding-box-only or case-level label inputs). We also state explicitly that these specifications were authored by the study team following domain conventions and were not independently validated by external experts against a formal rubric, and we list independent expert review as future work.

We also state explicitly in the Discussion that the evaluation used representative tasks and a study-defined intent set, and that validation of methodological appropriateness across arbitrary study designs remains future work.
}

\Location{
Methods, Sections 2.4 and 2.5; Results, Section 3.2; Discussion.
}

\RevisedText{
Built-in quality-control functions then checked image completeness, annotation integrity, metadata consistency, and train--test leakage risk; the leakage checks included duplicate-identifier verification across splits.
}

\ReviewerComment{
The manuscript acknowledges the absence of broader workflow evaluation, formal usability testing, and formal evaluation of the reporting component. However, these are not peripheral areas for future work; they are central components of platform validity.
}

\AuthorResponse{
We agree that these limitations directly affect the strength of the conclusions. Because a formal usability study could not be completed within this revision cycle, we have addressed this comment by strictly aligning the claims with the available evidence rather than by overstating what was evaluated.

Specifically, all statements implying a demonstrated reduction of the technical barrier have been removed or reframed as design intent (``is designed to reduce the coordination burden of multi-step dental AI experiments''). The manually operated comparison is now explicitly reported as a workflow demonstration rather than a controlled human-factor experiment. The reporting component is described through its structured, review-oriented outputs and the audit trail it forms together with the plan file, QC decisions, and execution logs.

The absence of a formal usability experiment with clinicians or dental researchers is now explicitly listed among the limitations, together with the study-defined nature of the evaluation set, and user-facing validation is identified as necessary future work.
}

\Location{
Abstract; Methods, Section 2.1; Results, Sections 3.1 and 3.3; Discussion.
}

\ReviewerComment{
The clinical terminology is inconsistent and overstated. The figures and text use terms such as ``clinical summary'', ``clinical review'', and ``clinical impact'', while the manuscript also states that the reporting layer is not a validated clinical decision-support tool.
}

\AuthorResponse{
We agree and have systematically revised the terminology as follows:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item ``clinician-facing workflow platform'' was replaced with ``researcher-oriented workflow platform'';
    \item ``clinician-facing interpretation'' in the report figure caption was replaced with ``review-oriented interpretation'';
    \item ``expert review'' was replaced with ``downstream review'';
    \item the Clinical Significance section was rewritten to describe the design intent of reducing the coordination burden of multi-step dental AI experiments rather than any demonstrated clinical benefit.
\end{itemize}

Figures 1 and 2 have additionally been redrawn with research- and review-oriented terminology throughout (e.g., ``Clinical \& Research Use'' $\rightarrow$ ``Research \& Review Use'', ``Clinical Summary'' $\rightarrow$ ``Workflow Summary'', ``CLINICAL IMPACT'' $\rightarrow$ ``WORKFLOW OUTPUTS''), and the Reporting Agent description in Figure 1 now reads ``research- and review-oriented digital dental workflows''. The representative report screenshot (Figure 4) was regenerated from the updated reporting template and uses neutral, research-oriented wording.

