}

\Location{
Abstract; Introduction; revised Figures 1, 2, and 4; Discussion; Conclusions.
}

\RevisedText{
By standardizing workflow execution and traceability, DentalClaw is designed to reduce the coordination burden of multi-step dental AI experiments while preserving a reviewable record of the data, configuration, and outputs associated with dental AI studies.
}

\ReviewerComment{
Errors embedded in a workflow-generation platform may propagate across multiple downstream studies and thereby amplify methodological defects. Platform-level validation therefore carries a greater responsibility.
}

\AuthorResponse{
We agree. The revised manuscript documents a fail-safe execution policy that constrains error propagation: only methods present in the offline registry can be executed automatically; external proposals require explicit user confirmation before any execution is attempted; and all workflow steps are performed by local, versioned execution modules rather than by the reasoning agent.

The reasoning backend is invoked at a low sampling temperature (0.1) and is constrained to return a structured JSON decision, with a parsing fallback for fenced or malformed responses. The decision evaluation additionally includes trap and boundary requests for which non-execution is the correct behaviour, and the results show that no unvalidated workflow was silently executed in any evaluated case.

We further added an explicit threat-model description: the reasoning agent is treated as an external, potentially untrusted component whose output is validated input rather than executable code; if the agent is unavailable or its decision cannot be parsed, the platform falls back to deterministic registry lookup and rejects the request when no validated method matches; retrieved literature is used only as auxiliary context, never as executable instruction; externally sourced code is reviewed before adaptation; and API credentials are read from environment variables and are not embedded in prompts or stored in workflow logs. Residual risks, including prompt-injection attacks mediated by retrieved content or malicious file metadata and supply-chain risks of externally sourced code, are explicitly acknowledged in the Discussion.
}

\Location{
Methods, Sections 2.1 and 2.2; Results, Section 3.2; Discussion.
}

\RevisedText{
The platform additionally applied a fail-safe execution policy: only methods present in the offline registry could be executed automatically, external proposals required an explicit user confirmation before any execution was attempted, and the workflow steps themselves were performed by local, versioned execution modules rather than by the reasoning agent.
}

\ReviewerComment{
The study should be repositioned as a platform-validation study and should provide evidence that DentalClaw generates appropriate, reproducible, auditable, and methodologically defensible workflows.
}

\AuthorResponse{
We have adopted this recommendation.

The title, abstract, study objective, Methods, order of the Results, Discussion, and Conclusions have all been rewritten around workflow-platform validation, and all claims are explicitly restricted to the evaluated workflow space.

The revised evidence chain includes:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item a documented five-stage agent decision pipeline;
    \item a prespecified 30-request intent-to-workflow decision evaluation with predefined expected outcomes and a same-rubric comparison against three general-purpose assistants;
    \item built-in quality-control and leakage checks with deterministic case-level splitting;
    \item a fail-safe execution policy with explicit human approval for external proposals;
    \item task-level execution case studies for 2D panoramic and 3D CBCT segmentation; and
    \item an audit trail formed by the plan file, QC decisions, execution logs, and generated outputs.
\end{itemize}

Statements that could not be supported by the available evidence have been removed or reframed as design intent, and the remaining gaps are explicitly acknowledged in the Discussion.
}

\Location{
The entire manuscript, particularly the Title, Abstract, final paragraph of the Introduction, Methods Sections 2.1--2.5, Results Sections 3.1--3.3, Discussion, and Conclusions.
}

