}

\AuthorResponse{
We clarified the positioning of DentalClaw relative to existing frameworks and corrected the associated citations. nnU-Net, MONAI, and 3D Slicer are now cited separately as model-development and imaging frameworks, whereas DentalClaw is positioned as a complementary orchestration layer that translates natural-language research intent into candidate execution routes, records actions in an auditable form, and enforces a conservative execution policy.

The Discussion makes the distinction explicit: frameworks such as nnU-Net and MONAI provide building blocks for model training and image processing, whereas DentalClaw addresses the decision and audit problem of which workflow should be executed for a given research intent, with which dataset, and under which quality-control safeguards.
}

\Location{
Introduction, paragraphs 3--4; Discussion, paragraph 1.
}

\RevisedText{
DentalClaw addresses a complementary need: it orchestrates the experimental workflow around user intent, converts natural-language requests into candidate executable routes, and records actions in an auditable form.
}

\ReviewerComment{
