\ReviewerComment{
The manuscript lacks sufficient details regarding large language model orchestration, prompt design, hallucination or error handling, and failure cases.
}

\AuthorResponse{
We added a dedicated subsection describing the large language model decision pipeline and its constraints.

The reasoning backend (DeepSeek V4 Pro) is invoked at a low sampling temperature (0.1) and is constrained to return a structured JSON decision, with a parsing fallback for fenced or malformed responses. The agent may select only registered methods or propose an external solution; it cannot execute code itself. A fail-safe execution policy ensures that only offline-registry methods execute automatically, that external proposals require explicit user confirmation, and that workflow steps are performed by local, versioned execution modules. Literature search over Europe PMC and arXiv serves as auxiliary context for proposal generation and reference citation, whereas registry-based route selection remains deterministic and does not depend on live search results.

Failure behaviour is demonstrated in the decision evaluation: ambiguous and trap requests are handled by clarification or rejection, and boundary requests without a validated route never lead to silent execution.
}

\Location{
Methods, Sections 2.1 and 2.2; Results, Section 3.2.
}

\RevisedText{
The decision layer used the OpenClaw runtime with DeepSeek V4 Pro as the primary reasoning backend (sampling temperature 0.1), constrained to return a structured JSON decision with a parsing fallback for malformed responses. Prompts contained only textual task descriptors, registry metadata, and codebase asset descriptions; raw images, annotations, and patient identifiers were processed locally and were never transmitted to the model API.
}

\ReviewerComment{
The comparison with manually operated scripts is insufficiently described. The authors should state who performed the manual workflow, how many operators were involved, and their level of expertise.
}

\AuthorResponse{
We revised the description of the manual comparison and, importantly, reclassified what it can support.

In answer to the specific questions raised: the manual arm was performed by a single operator, the AI researcher on the study team (a practitioner in the field, and the same operator who performed the user-selected configuration comparison reported in the Supplementary Methods), who was familiar with the datasets and tooling and followed the same predefined workflow without additional hyperparameter tuning or model selection. Both arms were timed as elapsed wall-clock time on the same NVIDIA RTX 3090 workstation.

The comparison is now explicitly reported as a workflow demonstration rather than a controlled human-factor experiment, both in the text and in the caption of Supplementary Table S1. The revised text emphasises the reduction in operator coordination during dataset preparation and result organisation, notes that model training time remained essentially unchanged, and draws no conclusions about statistical efficiency or usability. Claims that the manual comparison demonstrates human-factor benefits have been removed.
}

\Location{
Results, Section 3.1; Supplementary Table S1 and the Manual comparison protocol.
}

\RevisedText{
In the documented runs, the platform required substantially less operator coordination for dataset preparation and result organization, whereas model training time remained essentially unchanged, consistent with its role as a coordination layer rather than a training accelerator.
}

\ReviewerComment{
The artificial intelligence implementation details are insufficient. Important parameters such as the number of training epochs, learning rate, and preprocessing procedures are not adequately described.
}

\AuthorResponse{
We have now documented the complete training configuration in the Supplementary Methods. All models were trained with nnU-Net v2 using the framework's default training recipe unless stated otherwise: stochastic gradient descent with Nesterov momentum, polynomial learning-rate decay from an initial rate of 0.01, and a combined cross-entropy and soft Dice loss. The 32-class panoramic trajectory used the U-Mamba architecture trained for 100 epochs, with Gaussian-noise and gamma-correction augmentation at training time, a cosine-annealed learning-rate schedule, the AdamW optimizer (initial learning rate $1\times10^{-4}$, weight decay $1\times10^{-4}$), and mirroring test-time augmentation; the 2D configuration used training patches of 896$\times$1536 pixels at 1.0 mm spacing with a batch size of 2 and Z-score intensity normalization, and the 35-class CBCT models used the 3D full-resolution configuration with patches of 96$\times$160$\times$128 voxels at 0.3 mm isotropic spacing and a batch size of 2. Preprocessing and splitting details, the complete configuration trajectory tables, and the software environment are reported in the Supplementary Methods, and all configuration choices remain part of the audit trail described in Section 3.3.
}

\Location{
Methods, Section 2.4; Supplementary Methods (Training configuration details and Software environment); Results, Section 3.3.
}

